KG42.register("rules-aa6220b4d1c97c6ec75f0500",{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R009","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R009","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Prosumer participation","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"Prosumers may participate in peer-to-peer microgrid trading of self-produced energy. Scope: Proposed general microgrid energy market design. The market participant population must include a subgroup capable of producing energy; small prosumers with PV systems are an example. Scope: General microgrid setup: generation-capable participation. Permitted participants include local prosumers, mostly with PV systems, and hyper-local prosumers. Scope: BMG virtual market participant categories.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function participation.prosumer under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["participation.prosumer"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"BMG virtual market participant categories. | General microgrid setup: generation-capable participation. | Proposed general microgrid energy market design.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g03-e002"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Prosumers may participate in peer-to-peer microgrid trading of self-produced energy. Scope: Proposed general microgrid energy market design.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E01","v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E03"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The market participant population must include a subgroup capable of producing energy; small prosumers with PV systems are an example. Scope: General microgrid setup: generation-capable participation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Permitted participants include local prosumers, mostly with PV systems, and hyper-local prosumers. Scope: BMG virtual market participant categories.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e002","v141-b-g02-e02","v141-b-g02-e03","v141-b-g03-e001"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p01-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"n Rock b, Scott Kessler b, Lawrence Orsini b, ting, Fritz-Erler-Str. 23, 76133 Karlsruhe, Germany ma","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In our approach to tackle these challenges, we suggest that consumers and prosumers can trade self-produced energy in a peer-to-peer fashion on microgrid energy markets. Thus, consumers and prosumers can keep profits from energy trading within their community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Microgrid setup (C1)","anchor_text":"A clear objective, the deﬁnition of market participants, and the w- form of energy traded must be de","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A clear objective, the definition of market participants, and the form of energy traded must be defined. Microgrids can pursue several, often conflicting objectives [27]. Exemplary objectives are the increase of the security of energy supply or the integration of local renewable generation into the energy supply system. The implementation of the objectives is especially apparent in the design of the pricing method (C4). A microgrid energy market requires a sufficient number of market participants trading energy amongst each other. A subgroup of them needs to have the ability to produce energy. In microgrids this may happen in the form of small prosumers with PV systems [31]. Market access should only be given to the communities’ residents or similar defined groups of market participants. The form of energy traded in the microgrid needs to be defined, e.g. electricity, heat, or a combination thereof. Agents are usually assumed to be self-interested and rational [29]. Additionally, the microgrid setup needs to define whether the traditional energy grid is used for energy transport or a physical microgrid is build."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e002","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y g g g gy p system, and empowering the community. Market participants are deﬁned as local prosumers","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Market participants are defined as local prosumers (mostly with PV systems), consumers, and hyper-local participants (consumers, prosumers, and (possibly) producers). In addition to trading electricity, heat will be integrated in the future."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p01-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"1. Introduction","anchor_text":"this environmental energy dilemma [3]. Nevertheless, uncertainty and ﬂuctuation in renewable generat","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Microgrid energy markets allow small-scale participants, i.e. consumers and prosumers (consumers that also produce energy), to actively trade energy within their community in (near) real time. Thus, they facilitate a sustainable, reliable, and local balance of generation and consumption."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.1 Microgrid energy markets","anchor_text":"ide provide economic beneﬁts [3]. rgy Traditional centralized energy systems can be characterized by","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Microgrid energy markets provide small-scale prosumers and consumers with a market platform to trade locally generated energy within their community. Hence, they promote the consumption of energy close to its generation and, therefore, foster sustainability and the efficient use of local resources. An exemplary microgrid energy market scenario of residential consumers and prosumers (consumers with photovoltaic (PV) systems) is shown in Fig. 2. Note, that market participants do not necessarily have to be physically connected. Virtual microgrids are the aggregated control of multiple energy producers, prosumers, and consumers in a virtual community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Microgrid setup (C1)","anchor_text":"A clear objective, the deﬁnition of market participants, and the w- form of energy traded must be de","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A clear objective, the deﬁnition of market participants, and the form of energy traded must be deﬁned. Microgrids can pursue several, often conﬂicting objectives [27]. Exemplary objectives are the increase of the security of energy supply or the integration of local renewable generation into the energy supply system. The implementation of the objectives is especially apparent in the design of the pricing method (C4). A microgrid energy market requires a sufﬁcient number of market participants trading energy amongst each other. A subgroup of them needs to have the ability to produce energy. In microgrids this may happen in the form of small prosumers with PV systems [31]. Market access should only be given to the communities’ residents or similar deﬁned groups of market participants. The form of energy traded in the microgrid needs to be deﬁned, e.g. electricity, heat, or a combination thereof. Agents are usually assumed to be self-interested and rational [29]. Additionally, the microgrid setup needs to define whether the traditional energy grid is used for energy transport or a physical microgrid is build."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"y p pp y y g g g y thermore, it empowers the community to take over responsibility for their power s","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Market participants are local consumers and prosumers (C1). Hyper-local participation is also feasible. While only electricity may be traded for now, heat will be included in the future."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y g g g gy p system, and empowering the community. Market participants are deﬁned as local prosumers","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Market participants are deﬁned as local prosumers (mostly with PV systems), consumers, and hyper-local participants (consumers, prosumers, and (possibly) producers). In addition to trading electricity, heat will be integrated in the future."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e001","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"C1 U The BMG clearly aims at increasing the local security of energy supply and at strengthening the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 1","image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The BMG clearly aims at increasing the local security of energy supply and at strengthening the community and the integration of local RES. The market participants are local and hyper-local consumers and prosumers."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"No decision variable is defined in the supplied evidence or frozen graph. The material states who may participate and what participant categories are permitted; it does not define an operational choice or design selection represented as x. The only graph member is the unary relation participation.prosumer.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed parameter, numeric threshold, coefficient, or unit is supplied. The evidence uses qualitative terms such as a 'sufficient number' of participants and a 'subgroup' with production capability; no threshold or parameter value may be invented."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E01","quote":"consumers and prosumers can trade self-produced energy in a peer-to-peer fashion on microgrid energy markets."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","quote":"A microgrid energy market requires a sufficient number of market participants trading energy amongst each other. A subgroup of them needs to have the ability to produce energy. In microgrids this may happen in the form of small prosumers with PV systems [31]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e002","quote":"Market participants are defined as local prosumers (mostly with PV systems), consumers, and hyper-local participants (consumers, prosumers, and (possibly) producers)."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"4fad3e8f926fe0a109f2b71736430b90612327e3af8c0014906bafb5860897ff","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"Rule R009 consists of source-stated participant eligibility and design requirements: prosumers may trade self-produced energy, a producing subgroup is required, and BMG participant categories include local and hyper-local prosumers. The frozen graph provides only the unary relation participation.prosumer, with no edges, variables, parameters, quantities, thresholds, or equations. The supplied evidence therefore supports a procedural admission/participation rule, not a mathematical optimization or formula. The three phi entries remain faithfully not_formalized rather than inventing set notation, membership predicates, or numeric thresholds not represented in the frozen graph.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C001 states that prosumers may participate by trading self-produced energy. The frozen graph represents this with the unary relation participation.prosumer; R009 has no edges in E."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-C002","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 requires a generation-capable participant subgroup, illustrated by PV prosumers. This is represented by the same unary participation.prosumer relation; no edge or parameter exists in the frozen R009 graph for a numerical sufficiency threshold."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R009-C003","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C003 identifies local and hyper-local prosumers as permitted BMG participant categories. The frozen graph provides only participation.prosumer as the relevant unary relation and no edges."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
